(Test timer start time / end time - plan 250 D3: `time` is a reserved word
 and tokenised before the multi-word property handler could match, so these
 properties were unreachable even though the parser implements them)

a timer called "t".
Start t.
Wait 50 milliseconds.
Stop t.

(Test 1: two-word properties on an unquoted name)
a number called "st" is t's start time.
a number called "et" is t's end time.
If et is greater than or equal to st then,
    Print "two-word ok".
Otherwise,
    Print "bad".

(Test 2: the one-word forms still work)
a number called "s2" is t's start.
a number called "e2" is t's end.
If e2 is greater than or equal to s2 then,
    Print "one-word ok".
Otherwise,
    Print "bad".

(Test 3: the documented quoted multi-word name form from LANGUAGE.md)
Create a timer called "job timer".
Start the "job timer".
Wait 50 milliseconds.
Stop the "job timer".
a number called "js" is the "job timer"'s start time.
a number called "je" is the "job timer"'s end time.
If je is greater than or equal to js then,
    Print "quoted ok".
Otherwise,
    Print "bad".
